---
import ProjectLayout  from '@/layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import ProjectSection from '@/components/ProjectSection.astro';
import ProjectGallery from '@/components/ProjectGallery.astro';
import SpecTable      from '@/components/SpecTable.astro';
import Credits        from '@/components/Credits.astro';
import { getProject, getNeighbours } from '@/data/projects';

const p = getProject('j2-launcher');
---

<ProjectLayout
  title={p.title}
  subtitle="Builds"
  role={p.role}
  year={p.year}
  tags={p.tags}
  overview={p.summary}
  ogImage={p.cover}
  {...getNeighbours(p.slug)}
>

  <ProjectSection num="01" title="From Javelin 2 to J2" dark>
    <p>
      It began as <strong>Javelin 2</strong>, a three-person AP Physics 1
      project. I was lead designer, planner and builder: a compressed-air
      launcher on a wooden A-frame, firing T-shirts and tennis balls from a PVC
      barrel.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then I rebuilt it so it aims itself. Click a target in the camera view
      and <strong>J2</strong> solves for the launch angle and pressure that put
      a projectile there, then drives the barrel to that solution on two
      stepper-driven axes.
    </p>

    <ProjectGallery
      cols={2}
      images={[
        {
          src: '/images/cannon-6.jpg',
          alt: 'The original Javelin 2 launcher on its wooden A-frame outdoors with its three student builders',
          caption: 'Javelin 2 as built for AP Physics 1 — Avi Amin, Benjamin Davis and Liam Sheehan.',
        },
        {
          src: '/images/projects/j2-complete.jpg',
          alt: 'The rebuilt J2 launcher standing on its rotating base outdoors, barrel raised against autumn woods',
          focus: 'center 45%',
          caption: 'The camera-aimed rebuild, on its pan turntable.',
        },
      ]}
    />
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="02" title="The valve">
    <p>
      A launcher is only as fast as the thing letting the air out. A hardware-store
      ball valve takes most of a second to open, and by the time it is fully
      open the pressure behind the projectile has already collapsed.
    </p>
    <p>
      J2 uses a <strong>modified sprinkler diaphragm valve</strong> instead.
      Venting the chamber above the diaphragm dumps the full bore in
      milliseconds rather than seconds, so the projectile sees something much
      closer to the stored pressure.
    </p>

    <ProjectGallery
      cols={2}
      images={[
        {
          src: '/images/projects/j2-valve.jpg',
          alt: 'The sprinkler diaphragm valve body opened up, showing the brass outlet and the internal seat',
          caption: 'The diaphragm valve, opened for modification.',
        },
        {
          src: '/images/projects/j2-muzzle.jpg',
          alt: 'Looking down the PVC barrel of the launcher toward the muzzle, bore in sharp focus',
          caption: 'Down the bore.',
        },
      ]}
    />
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="03" title="Aiming and controls" dark>
    <p>
      The aiming stack is deliberately split: a vision board that finds the
      target, a microcontroller that moves the axes, and a physical controller
      that a person has to hold to make anything fire.
    </p>

    <SpecTable
      caption="Aiming and control hardware"
      rows={[
        { key: 'Camera',      value: 'Limelight 3A' },
        { key: 'Compute',     value: 'Raspberry Pi' },
        { key: 'Motion control', value: 'SparkFun RedBoard' },
        { key: 'Axes',        value: 'Two stepper-driven axes — elevation and pan' },
        { key: 'Pan drive',   value: 'Turntable on a segmented 3D-printed ring gear' },
        { key: 'Manual mode', value: 'LCD and joystick for direct aiming' },
        { key: 'Fire control', value: 'Handheld controller, designed and wired by me' },
      ]}
    />

    <p>
      The pan axis is the part I am happiest with. A ring gear that size is not
      something a desktop printer can produce in one piece, so it is printed in
      segments that bolt into a continuous ring around a plywood turntable, and
      driven by a pinion on a stepper.
    </p>

    <ProjectGallery
      cols={3}
      images={[
        {
          src: '/images/projects/j2-turntable.jpg',
          alt: 'The plywood pan turntable clamped on the bench with its segmented teal ring gear fitted around the rim',
          caption: 'The segmented ring gear, closed into a continuous ring.',
        },
        {
          src: '/images/projects/j2-electronics.jpg',
          alt: 'A SparkFun RedBoard, Raspberry Pi, breadboard and terminal blocks laid out on the bench before wiring',
          caption: 'Control hardware laid out before wiring.',
        },
        {
          src: '/images/projects/j2-controller.jpg',
          alt: 'The handheld fire controller held in one hand — arcade buttons and a large red stop button in a printed enclosure',
          caption: 'The handheld fire controller, with its stop button.',
        },
      ]}
    />
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="04" title="Projectiles and testing">
    <p>
      Golf and tennis balls fly from custom sabots I modeled and printed in
      PLA, PETG-CF and TPU — the softer TPU sabots survive repeat firings that
      split the rigid ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tracking was tested against a fluorescent target carried by a drone,
      <strong>with the launcher depressurized</strong>: the aiming system
      followed the target and drove the axes to the firing solution while the
      machine itself could not fire.
    </p>
    <p>
      Muzzle velocity and range were measured with Vernier Video Analysis.
      I am re-running that measurement before publishing a figure, so no
      performance numbers appear on this page yet.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="05" title="Credits" dark>
    <Credits
      teamLabel="Original AP Physics 1 team"
      team={[
        { name: 'Avi Amin', url: 'https://aviamin.com' },
        { name: 'Liam Sheehan' },
        { name: 'Benjamin Davis' },
      ]}
      items={[
        'Inspired by the Duke tennis ball cannon.',
        'Diaphragm valve modification follows a Spudfiles guide.',
      ]}
      aiNote="The firmware and aiming software were generated with Claude Code from my prompt and used as generated. I designed, built, wired and tested the hardware."
    />
  </ProjectSection>

</ProjectLayout>
